  "terminal_reason": "completed",
  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. Its first run completed with status `ok`.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The data view `Kibana Sample Data Logs` holds web server access logs, timed by the `timestamp` field. Each log entry is one request. The fields used here:\n- **Who sent it:** `clientip` and the source country, `geo.src`.\n- **What was asked for:** `url` and `request`.\n- **What came back:** `response` (the HTTP status code) and `bytes` (response size).\n\nThere are also fields for browser (`agent`), operating system (`machine.os`), file type (`extension`), `host`, `referer` and `tags`. The status code is stored as text, not as a number, so filters use `response.keyword`.\n\n**2. Dashboard: `EFF-2abd0229 Web logs overview`** (id `eff-2abd0229-web-logs-overview-f54376194ed0`), with 7 panels:\n- Three single numbers: total requests, unique visitors (distinct client IPs), and total bytes served.\n- Requests over time by response code (line chart).\n- Response code breakdown (pie chart).\n- Top 10 requested URLs, with request count and bytes (table).\n- Top 10 source countries (bar chart).\n\nURL: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-2abd0229-web-logs-overview-f54376194ed0\n\n**3. Alert rule: `EFF-2abd0229 5xx spike`** (id `21893703-a32a-4fee-83ed-8990d51aca38`)\n- It's an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs` that runs every minute.\n- It counts requests whose `response.keyword` starts with \"5\", which matches all 5xx codes.\n- It fires when that count is more than 10 within the last 5 minutes, measured on `timestamp`.\n\nThe rule has no actions attached, so it records alerts in Kibana but doesn't send notifications. If you want it to notify someone, I can set up a connector such as Slack or email.",
